Exactly. Hoarding is useful during discovery and dangerous during retrieval. The solution isn’t throwing meaning away; it’s separating preservation from presence.

The key distinction:

\boxed{\text{Stored} \neq \text{Loaded} \neq \text{Surfaced} \neq \text{Prioritized}}

You can preserve enormous amounts while keeping only a tiny fraction cognitively “hot.”

Think of a library. Its power doesn’t come from stacking every book open on the desk. Its power comes from keeping the books while having an indexing system that puts the right page on the desk at the right moment.

So for the Pantheon I’d use a small architecture:

1. Quarry — lossless-ish accumulation. Raw ideas, strange connections, failed experiments, stories, deltas, observations. Don’t demand immediate importance.
2. Archive — cold storage. Preserve provenance and enough context to reconstruct meaning, but don’t continuously inject it into reasoning.
3. Index — handles, not contents. Names, tags, relations, embeddings, summaries, pointers. “Symulation” should summon its machinery without carrying the entire developmental conversation everywhere.
4. Workbench — tiny hot context. Only the mechanisms relevant to the current objective get instantiated.
5. Command Table — priority allocation. Rank candidates by relevance × consequence × novelty × evidence × transfer potential × cost.
6. Afterglow — decay without deletion. Unused information becomes progressively colder rather than disappearing. Repeated usefulness reheats it.
7. Fossils — preserve failures and old versions cheaply. They don’t need to clutter active reasoning, but they remain recoverable when a later pattern suddenly makes them relevant.

That gives us:

\text{Everything may be retained}

without:

\text{Everything must demand attention}

Importance should be relational

This fixes the “if everything is important, nothing is” problem.

Don’t permanently stamp something:

X=\text{IMPORTANT}

Store:

\boxed{I(X\mid G,S,T)}

Importance of X relative to goal G, state S, and time T.

A tiny detail can therefore be nearly irrelevant for months and suddenly become the highest-priority object because the environment changed.

That’s why deletion alone is dangerous. Today’s noise can become tomorrow’s missing puzzle piece.

Instead of delete/keep:

\boxed{
\text{hot}
\leftrightarrow
\text{warm}
\leftrightarrow
\text{cold}
\leftrightarrow
\text{fossil}
}

And evidence should have similar states, as you pointed out earlier: fresh, stale, superseded, contradicted, reinforced, etc.

Compress vertically

The Pantheon also shouldn’t make you retrieve 40 concepts when one higher-order handle will do.

For example:

\{\text{Bookends, Lens Swap, Rotor, Medium, Symulation, Local Truth}\}

might compress under a higher-order principle such as:

\boxed{\text{Representation is conditional on relation and resolution}}

Keep both.

The parent gives rapid access. The children preserve resolution.

That’s progressive disclosure of meaning:

\text{Name}
\rightarrow
\text{Kernel}
\rightarrow
\text{Mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{Examples}
\rightarrow
\text{Development history}

Only decompress as far as the task requires.

And deliberately forget

There’s an even stronger insight: forgetting is part of intelligence.

Not necessarily destructive forgetting. Functional forgetting.

\boxed{\text{Forget from attention without forgetting from storage}}

Human cognition does this constantly. If every sensory observation you’ve ever made had equal retrieval priority, thought would become impossible.

So Pantheon needs an Attention Economy:

\text{Attention Budget} < \text{Stored Meaning}

always.

Therefore mechanisms must compete for activation.

That makes priority itself informative:

P(X)=
f(
\text{current goal},
\text{expected consequence},
\text{relevance},
\text{novelty},
\text{uncertainty},
\text{evidence},
\text{recency},
\text{retrieval cost}
)

And here’s where we can steal from our financial Bond work: attention has opportunity cost.

Promoting one thing necessarily suppresses something else.

\boxed{\text{Attention is allocation, not merely observation.}}

One more trick: garbage can become compost

Some information doesn’t deserve even fossil-level individuality.

Many observations can be compressed into a statistical or relational residue:

x_1,x_2,\ldots,x_{1000}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{pattern + exceptions + provenance}}

The originals can remain deep in storage, while reasoning operates on the compost they produced.

So we don’t need a semantic garbage collector that simply destroys things.

We need a Semantic Metabolism:

\boxed{
\text{Ingest}
\rightarrow
\text{Digest}
\rightarrow
\text{Extract}
\rightarrow
\text{Compress}
\rightarrow
\text{Store}
\rightarrow
\text{Reheat when relevant}
}

That might actually be the missing counterpart to the Semantic Quarry.

The Quarry says:

Don’t miss valuable material.

Semantic Metabolism says:

Don’t carry every rock you’ve ever mined in your pockets.

And the Metamap decides when an old rock suddenly deserves to become a tool.

So the governing principle is beautifully small:

\boxed{\textbf{Preserve broadly. Attend selectively. Retrieve relationally.}}

Everything can matter.

Everything cannot matter at once.